  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1104 PM CST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nA tricky winter forecast is unfolding across the Mid-South at\nthis hour with 10PM radar imagery depicting a rain/snow line\nright along the Mississippi River. West of the river, moderate\nsnow showers are occurring with traffic cameras in northeast\nArkansas and the Missouri Bootheel already depicting a dusting of\nsnow. The changeover from rain to snow will continue to push east\novernight. The Memphis International Airport received several\nACARS soundings over the past few hours during the evening push.\nThese vertical profiles depict a shallow warm layer between\n800 and 900 mb. Each successive profile depicts cooling of this\nlayer, likely due to latent heat loss as hydrometeors melt. The\nRAP has a good handle of the current warm layer and depicts it\nfalling below freezing within an hour or two. By this time, mixed\nwinter precipitation is not out of the question for the Memphis\nmetropolitan area. Rain and snow showers will be the primary\nconcerns, but surface temperatures above freezing should keep\naccumulations to a minimum.\n\nElsewhere in the forecast area, there is growing concern for\nmoderate snow band development particularly in west Tennessee.\nThe REFS Snowband Probability Tracker is picking up on the\nongoing snow band formation and shows several members extending\nthis probability east of the current Winter Weather Advisory\narea. Given the present snow bands, along with the potential for\nmore overnight, the Winter Weather Advisory has been expanded to\ninclude additional counties in northeast Arkansas and west\nTennessee. The greatest snow totals will occur in Clay, Greene,\nPemiscot, and Dunklin counties. Up to  2.5 inches of snow is\nforecast in this area. Otherwise, a dusting of snow cannot be\nruled out in the remaining Advisory area. Winter precipitation\nwill continue into Thanksgiving morning. We urge all motorists to\nexercise extreme caution if traveling early Thursday. Ensure you\nobey traffic laws and leave extra room between you and the car\nahead of you. Luckily, temperatures will return to well above\nfreezing by midmorning. This will allow any leftover\nprecipitation to change over to rain. Showers will begin to pull\nout of the area Thursday night, leaving behind below-normal\ntemperatures into Friday.\n\nTemperatures this weekend will quickly rebound into the 60s by\nSaturday. Dry conditions will be short lived as a shortwave\ntrough ejects out of the Rockies and impacts the Mid-South early\nSunday. A few elevated thunderstorms are not out of the question\ninto Monday. Long term guidance depicts an active synoptic\npattern consisting of several shortwave passages through the end\nof next week. For now, it remains too early to tell specifics in\nregards to precipitation timing and amounts, but it is safe to\nassume another round of rainfall before the end of next week.\n\nANS",
